Filetype: Flipper RFID key
Version: 1
Frequency: 125000
Bit Count: 64
Key Type: EM4100
Key: DE AD BE EF CA
Data: DE AD BE EF CA

REM ==============================================================
REM  Audit_Trigger_Badge.rfid
REM
REM  Defensive-use RFID emulation. The UID `DE AD BE EF CA`
REM  is a deliberately impossible pattern -- the byte sequence
REM  `DE AD BE EF CA` cannot appear on a real-world EM4100 wafer,
REM  so this file CANNOT impersonate any access-control badge.
REM
REM  Twist vs. a vanilla cloned credential: this file is meant
REM  to be EMULATED by the Flipper at a desk-mounted RFID reader
REM  (or near an attached USB HF/LF reader like ACR122U) to
REM  trigger a known "audit mode" event. BugReaperX detects
REM  the impossible UID and reports:
REM    "Audit event: synthetic UID detected (DEADBEEF CA)"
REM  which is useful for:
REM    - Validating that an RFID-enabled workstation can detect
REM      emulated input at all.
REM    - Pen-test verification that the Flipper's RFID emulator
REM      is wired correctly (probe + reader handshake).
REM    - Smoke testing RFID log pipelines end-to-end without
REM      touching anyone else's badges.
REM
REM  EM4100 protocol summary:
REM    - 125 kHz carrier, ASK/OOK modulation.
REM    - 64-bit Manchester-coded transmission:
REM      10 sync + 8 version + 32 data (the 5 bytes above) +
REM      14 parity + 4 stop = approximately 64 bits.
REM    - The `Key:` field below encodes just the 32 data bits;
REM      the Flipper rebuilds the rest on the wire.
REM ==============================================================

REM Verify with:  python ..\scripts\verify_flipper_files.py
